#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:5
#: 861a750b172040cdac8f928e5edf2d41
msgid "Mounting the Autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:7
#: fe3beab942394562b3ad6186c51c9725
msgid "This article explains how to mount the autopilot in standard and alternative orientations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:11
#: bc4f05b5641643b88e711adbb58827ce
msgid "Standard orientation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:13
#: 91951efa35dd4be98a9c728e4c547fe5
msgid "The :ref:`autopilot <common-autopilots>` (Pixhawk etc.) should be housed within a case (which includes foam over the barometer) and should be mounted with the white arrow pointing directly towards the front of the vehicle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:18
#: cc495387cf9f4dc2835a61156ffaee22
msgid "It should be placed close to the center of gravity of the vehicle (both horizontally and vertically).  Generally this means it should be placed within a few centimeters of the middle of the vehicle and level with the motors.  It is not critical that it is placed exactly at the middle but closer is better (there are no recorded cases of problems caused by the controller being far from the centre of gravity).  If the autopilot cannot be placed at the center of the vehicle setting the :ref:`IMU position offset parameters <common-sensor-offset-compensation>` may improve performance slightly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:27
#: 124b19bbed804433a6d8a1887e57fffe
msgid "The board should be attached to the frame using 4 cubes of vibration dampening foam approximately 3/4 inch square (3M foam can be purchased from `mRobotics <https://store.mrobotics.io/product-p/mro-pxfoams-mr.htm>`__)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:36
#: 27de7fb0c9b64be6a51af8e07f09199c
msgid "Vibration dampening foam is a simple and effective mechanism for vibration control. If :ref:`Measuring Vibration <common-measuring-vibration>` shows that it is not sufficient, alternative mechanisms are discussed in :ref:`Vibration Damping <common-vibration-damping>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:41
#: ec38198b2f404fc99e97006826e10687
msgid "Alternative orientations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:43
#: 609517283eb747c4ab7a8c3e8e41d718
msgid "If the standard orientation of the Autopilot is not convenient, it can be mounted in over 30 other possible orientations.  The full list of possible orientations can be seen and set through the Mission Planner **Config/Tuning \\| Advanced Params** screen, look for the ``Board Orientation (AHRS_ORIENTATION)`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:49
#: ea4ca9ac474446f7888b1d1e2410f9c7
msgid "For Roll values, positive means roll right (i.e. \"Roll90\" means board is rolled right 90 degrees so the white arrow still points forward but the board is standing on its right edge)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:53
#: e41cec5646a1469ab1e0526b98d6ec72
msgid "For Pitch values, positive numbers indicate pitch back (i.e. \"Pitch90\" means the board's nose is pitched up 90 degrees so the arrow points directly up)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:57
#: 5f2f488c4df4492fa2b034dde75833ea
msgid "For Yaw values, positive numbers indicate the nose of the board is rotated clockwise (i.e. \"Yaw90\" means board is rotated so white arrow points off to the right)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-mounting-the-flight-controller.rst:64
#: e4ca8ce58c7c417e899973c7992f6dde
msgid "If the board is mounted in a non-standard orientation (i.e. arrow is not pointing forward) then please ensure the :ref:`AHRS_ORIENTATION<AHRS_ORIENTATION>`  is properly set before doing the accelerometer calibration."
msgstr ""
